Let a frontend with no window follow the lines it was shown

lineShown sends each line of the main console once its triggers have run, and never again. A script that later
changes such a line where it stands (moving the cursor up to it and calling replace(), setFgColor(), setLink() or
insertText()) or deletes it with deleteLine() changes the GUI's console, which repaints, while a frontend with no
window keeps showing the line as it was sent.

- TBuffer gives each line an id (lineIdBuffer), which a line keeps while lines around it are deleted or trimmed, and
  the first of the lines a rewrap splits it into keeps too.
- TConsoleModelNotifier::linesRemoved tells, before they go, of lines deleteLine() deletes or a shrink trims. Like
  lineShown, it is sent only when connected, which the GUI never does.
- lineShown gains a last argument, lineIds: an id for each line of its text, as its line feeds part it.
- THeadlessSession::linesUpdated sends, once a turn from the event loop, each of those lines a script has since
  changed (linesChanged) or deleted, as lineShown would send it now, or as deleted. Lines trimmed off the top and
  lines before a clearWindow() are dropped without one.
- docs/headless.md describes it.

Tested by Mudlet/android's native/tests/test_mudlet_engine.py (line_updates: a line replace() changed, one setFgColor()
restyled and one deleteLine() deleted, from runLua and from a trigger on a later line, sent again by seq; the last
line a timer deletes; and a UI that attaches sent the changes it missed).
The desktop is unchanged: it never connects linesRemoved or the session's signals, and the line ids only ride along
in the buffer.

Carried by Mudlet/android's scripts/apply-mudlet-patches.sh until Mudlet/Mudlet has it.
Upstream: not opened yet
